  "out_of_scope_findings": [
    "to file (class c, dedupe words: `PercentFieldMetadata scale missing` / `percent field metadata precision only` / `types field-types percent scale` / `PercentFieldMetadata precision declared` / `percent metadata scale undeclared`): `PercentFieldMetadata` in `@object-ui/types` declares `precision?: number` and NOT `scale`, while `NumberFieldMetadata` beside it declares both plus `step`. An author - an AI author especially - reading the published interface is offered exactly the member that no longer does anything in this widget, and the member it now reads is undeclared; the two detail hosts that forward `scale` have to cast to reach it. Plausibly the root cause of this very card. NOT folded in: widening an exported type would void `Clause-2: no` (ruling 3), and `packages/types` is outside the declared file surface.",
    "to file (class a, dedupe words: `scale above 100 RangeError` / `toFixed digits out of range percent` / `maximumFractionDigits out of range` / `spec scale no upper bound` / `percent render throws large scale`): a spec-valid `scale` above 100 crashes the render on BOTH percent faces. `FieldSchema` accepts `scale` as any non-negative integer with no upper bound, while MEASURED on node 22 `(25).toFixed(101)` throws `RangeError: toFixed() digits argument must be between 0 and 100` and `new Intl.NumberFormat('en', { maximumFractionDigits: 101 })` throws `RangeError: maximumFractionDigits value is out of range` - so `PercentField` throws and `PercentCellRenderer` throws. Pre-existing and unchanged in exposure by this branch (the trigger moves from `precision` to `scale`); not repaired because one ruling should cover both faces and neither a clamp nor a refusal is this widget's to invent (the spec's own note refuses consumer-invented semantics for a malformed declaration).",
    "to file (class b, dedupe words: `percent scale fraction storage magnitude` / `max_scale percent fraction 100x` / `percentScaleOf scale display` / `percent scale stored vs displayed` / `record validator max_scale percent widget step`): `scale` means STORED decimals to the write path and DISPLAYED decimals to the display path, 100x apart for a fraction-stored percent. A percent field stores a 0-1 fraction unless it declares `max > 1` (`percentScaleOf` in `@objectstack/spec`, whose numeric-column note states 33.333% reaches the column as 0.33333 - i.e. `scale: 5`), and the record validator judges `scale` against that fraction, while objectui#9295's landed and pinned convention applies `scale` to the percentage-point value. So a fraction-stored field declaring `scale: 2` now displays and steps in hundredths of a percentage point while the platform refuses any write finer than one whole percent (`max_scale`). This branch deliberately follows the landed display convention rather than re-litigating objectui#9295 (triage wrote the fold refusal verbatim), so the widget agrees with the cell beside it; the divergence is real and spans both repositories, and the decision belongs to whoever rules on it.",
    "noted, not filed: the absent-`scale` disagreement between this widget (`12.35%`) and `PercentCellRenderer` (`12%`). A design question with a real cost either way rather than a defect, and the card names it as part of the question. Successor: the queued same-convention siblings objectui#9574 (`plugin-form`) and objectui#9575 (`plugin-list`) are the next seats in this convention family and would be re-priced against any ruling that settles it.",
    "noted, not filed: two prose repairs inside the file this branch already edits - the widget's top doc comment said 'configurable decimal precision' and now names `scale`, and the recorded Chromium measurement said it was driven with `precision: 2` and now says 'a two-decimal width', because the field that produces that width is no longer `precision`. Neither restates a measurement that was not taken. Successor: this file's next editor."
  ]
